# Straight-line fit to 40 noisy points

## Task
Fit y = a + b·x to 40 points, x = 0, 1, …, 39, generated as y = 2.5 + 0.8·x + e, with e ~ N(0, 3²) drawn by `numpy.random.default_rng(217)` (one call, `rng.normal(0, 3, 40)`). The true values (intercept 2.5, slope 0.8) are known from the generator and are used only as a sanity check.

## Method
Ordinary least squares (`scipy.stats.linregress`). 95% confidence intervals are classical t intervals, estimate ± t(0.975, 38)·SE with t = 2.0244 (n − 2 = 38 degrees of freedom).

## Results
All values are copied from `results/fit.json` (experiment-repo, produced by `code/fit.py`).

| Quantity | Estimate | SE | 95% CI | True value | CI covers truth |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Slope | 0.8140 | 0.0423 | [0.7283, 0.8997] | 0.8 | yes |
| Intercept | 1.9142 | 0.9590 | [−0.0273, 3.8557] | 2.5 | yes |

- R² = 0.9068 (r = 0.9523).
- Residual SD = 3.090 (generator SD 3).
- Fitted line: ŷ = 1.914 + 0.814·x.

The slope is estimated precisely (CI half-width about 0.086). The intercept CI is wide (half-width about 1.94) because x = 0 lies far from the centre of the data, and it only just includes 0 (lower end −0.027), so the intercept is borderline against zero at the 5% level.

